**Johan's decision on [#37](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/37): "build it for v1."** The SPEC §14 harder case is in scope for v1, so this ticket builds it.

## The gap being closed

When Johan hijacks a **registered ticket session** and does out-of-scope work there, neither existing retro signal fires. Git attributes the commits to that ticket (the session is registered, the worktree is the ticket's), and the session's digest already carries a ticket, so the transcript signal sees nothing anomalous. Only *unregistered* sessions and *ticket-less* implementation trip the current detectors.

Closing it needs a judgment the current signals never ask for: **"is this diff what the ticket asked for?"** — the work compared against the ticket's *stated scope*, not merely the existence of a ticket. That is a brain call, not a structural one.

## Scope

**EXTEND `src/eyes/retro/`, do not duplicate it.** The signal → compose → tracker-filing plumbing already exists and already files retro-labeled sub-issues under the right parent through the `gh` seam. This is a new *signal* that plugs into it.

1. **The scope-drift signal.** For a session that IS registered to a ticket: gather the session's actual diff (the existing git signal already maps worktrees to parent repos — reuse it) plus the ticket's stated scope (title + body via the same `gh` seam the filer uses), and ask the brain the scope question. Cap the diff the way the retro eye already caps its git evidence — a huge diff must degrade, never blow the prompt.
2. **The brain prompt + model/effort.** Use the watcher-brain defaults settled in [#9](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/9)/ADR-0002 (`gpt-5.6-luna`, `model_reasoning_effort=low`, read-only `codex exec`) through the shared brain seam and the cap — no new brain, no new model tier. Structured output, and mind the schema rule #35 found the hard way: **every property must appear in `required`** (optional-and-omitted invalidates the whole schema at the API, surfacing as a bare `exit 1`), so model optional fields as required-and-nullable.
3. **Tie-break / false-positive policy — state it explicitly in code and in the prompt.** A legitimate in-scope refactor must NOT be flagged. Required stance: the burden of proof is on *drift* — when the diff is a plausible way to accomplish the stated ticket (refactors, test updates, renames, dependency-of-the-work changes, fixing something that blocked the gates), the verdict is NOT drift. Flag only work that serves a *different* goal than the ticket states. Ambiguity resolves to silence: a false positive costs Johan's trust in every retro issue Argus files, while a missed drift costs one un-filed issue. Encode that asymmetry, and test both directions.
4. **Filing.** A confirmed drift becomes a retro sub-issue under the correct parent via the existing compose + tracker path, with a body that names the ticket, quotes its stated scope, and describes the out-of-scope work concretely. Tracker-less mode degrades the way the rest of the retro eye already does.
5. **SPEC amendment.** Update **§14** to state that v1 detects in-ticket scope drift (with the false-positive stance), and remove the corresponding out-of-scope line from **§12** if one is there. The spec must match what ships.

## Acceptance — a §13 scenario

Register a new scenario (or extend the retro slot the way #33 and #35 did — `registry.get(n)` and chain, **never replace a sibling's half**) proving the full case end to end: **fake-Johan hijacks a registered ticket session and does out-of-scope work → the retro sub-issue is filed, under the correct parent, with a body naming the drift.** The negative case is equally required: **an in-scope refactor in a registered ticket session files nothing.**

Note `src/harness/all-scenarios.ts` (landing with #36) composes every ticket's registrars and pins each contributing ticket's own assertion — register there too so this scenario is part of the single graded run.

## Edge cases

- Registered session, ticket **closed** mid-session — still judge it, don't crash on a closed ticket.
- Ticket body empty or a one-liner: too little stated scope to judge ⇒ NOT drift (silence), never a guess.
- Diff spanning several repos/worktrees — judge per parent repo.
- Brain unavailable or a schema miss ⇒ report and move on; a broken `codex` must never turn the pass into a retry storm or take the eye down (the pattern #35 established).
- Double-filing: re-running the pass over the same session must not file the same drift twice.

## Method and gates

Strict TDD (red test first) via `pocock-plus:tdd`, small commits. `bun install` first. Gates: `bun x tsc --noEmit` + `bun test` green. Zero runtime dependencies. Testing policy §13: temp dirs, stubbed brain and fake `gh` in unit tests — never real `~/.claude`, `~/.hermes`, real `~/argus` state, or the real tracker. **Drift hazard:** assert YOUR slot's status only, never whole-registry lists.

## Sequencing

Runs alongside/after [#36](https://github.com/JohanSardar/argus/issues/36) (final acceptance). Per Johan: if #36's acceptance completes first, the affected scenarios get re-run after this lands, so the final green includes it.
